iterOpT -- SEQ 601 (Tony, 2026-11-10): ITERATe is the parent of four fixed shapes, each an operator built by the default
action: ITERPLAIn (op iterateOn), ITERATTRs (iterateAttrs), ITERMEMBERs (iterateMembers), ITERBOTh (iterateBoth). Each has
two slots, the cursor and the source; the filter words hand nothing up and the shape's op carries the filter. The source
is an EXPRESSIOn, so a starred source is its deref instruct, and an operator in the source is read as part of the source.

Not run alone: pop.sh appends the row's statements and a sentinel; one process per row under an alarm. Each value reads:
[stdout, lines joined by |] r<REFUSED lines> adj<the adjacent-operands wording> pre<the prefix-position wording>
plus<Operator + cannot apply> x<exit> s<sentinel>.

IO-1  both filter words: TODAY walks the attributes only. INTENDED (Tony, SEQ 602 R3): attributes and members. BORN RED, NOT CURED.
IO-2  a filter with a starred source.
IO-3  an operator before the source: refused today as a list of adjacent operands, then as an operator in a prefix
      position (the source is an EXPRESSIOn now); either way the loop refuses and the next statement runs.
IO-4  an operator after the source: TODAY the iterate takes fsrc, walks it (1 2 3), and "+ 1" is a second statement that
      refuses; INTENDED the source is fsrc + 1, which refuses when the iterate runs, and nothing is walked.
IO-5  control: a source with no list refuses at run, the old road's wording.
IO-6  control: the filter words in the other order are no shape; the statement is not an iterate and ++ refuses.
IO-7  no cursor: TODAY "on" is read as the cursor and nothing refuses. INTENDED (Tony, SEQ 602 R3): refused by name, and the
      next statement runs. BORN RED, NOT CURED.
IO-8  top level through FILe, a members walk across two calls.
IO-9  a new-road action body: a members walk, then 6.

